# =============================================================================
# Kenyon Express: every environment variable the code actually reads
# =============================================================================
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development and fill in real values.
#
# This file is generated against HEAD by reading the code, not from memory.
# Every variable below has at least one read site in `src/`, `apps/`,
# `scripts/`, `e2e/` or a root config file, and that read site is named on the
# line above it. Nothing here is aspirational: if a variable is listed,
# something reads it. If something reads a variable, it is listed.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* and EXPO_PUBLIC_* are inlined into the client bundle at BUILD
# time. Changing one in Vercel means redeploying, not restarting, and a secret
# with that prefix is a leak that already happened. `src/lib/env.ts` refuses to
# boot when it sees NEXT_PUBLIC_*(SECRET|PASSWORD|SERVICE_ROLE|PRIVATE_KEY|API_KEY).
#
# Legend
#   [required]  production refuses to boot or the feature is broken without it
#   [optional]  degrades to the documented fallback, named on the same line
#   [tooling]   scripts, tests and CI only; never set in Vercel
#   [platform]  injected by Vercel or the CI runner; do not set by hand
#
# The boot-time contract lives in `src/lib/env.ts`. In production it hard-fails
# on any of: NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY,
# CARDCOM_TERMINAL_NUMBER, CARDCOM_API_NAME, CARDCOM_API_PASSWORD,
# CARDCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET, VOUCHER_QR_SECRET, CRON_SECRET, one of
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY / SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY, and CARDCOM_SANDBOX=true.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source for all of these: Supabase dashboard > Project Settings > API.

# [required] Read by: src/proxy.ts, src/lib/env.ts, src/lib/supabase/{client,server,admin}.ts
# Source: Supabase > Project Settings > API > Project URL.
# Trap, hit on 2026-07-28: pointing this at a local stack while that stack is
# down makes every page render as if the catalogue were empty, with no error.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co

# [required] Read by: src/lib/supabase/anon-key.ts (every anon client goes
# through it), src/lib/env.ts. Source: same page, the publishable / anon key.
# Safe in the browser by design; this is the value inlined into the bundle.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# [optional] Server-side override for the anon key, read FIRST by
# src/lib/supabase/anon-key.ts. Only needed during a key rotation: setting it
# moves every server-side client to the new key with an env change alone,
# while the NEXT_PUBLIC_ value above catches up on the next build. Remove it
# once the rotation is done. Procedure: docs/RUNBOOK.md, "Rotating the
# Supabase anon (publishable) key".
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# [required, one of these two] Read by: src/lib/supabase/admin.ts, src/lib/env.ts
# Source: same page, the SECRET key. Bypasses RLS. Never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is the legacy JWT-shaped key; SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY is
# the newer `sb_secret_...` form. src/lib/supabase/admin-key.ts accepts either,
# and src/lib/env.ts fails the boot when both are absent.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_...

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/supabase/timeout-fetch.ts (supabaseTimeoutMs)
# Milliseconds a single Supabase request may take. Fallback when unset: 10000.
# Read per call, not at module load, so a warm instance picks a change up.
# Anything that is not a positive integer falls back rather than disabling the
# timeout: a request with no ceiling holds the whole invocation open.
# SUPABASE_TIMEOUT_MS=10000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# App identity and URLs
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [required] Read by: src/app/layout.tsx, the invoice route, src/lib/payments/env.ts
# Source: your own canonical origin. Production: https://kenyonexpress.co.il
# loadCardcomEnv() throws without it outside mock mode: it is what the Cardcom
# IndicatorUrl and ReturnUrl are built from, so a wrong value breaks payment
# callbacks rather than just links.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# [required] Read by: src/app/api/wallet/apple/[id]/route.ts, src/components/coupon/WalletButtons.tsx
# Source: the same value as NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL. Two variables, both read, in
# different modules. Set them to the same thing and keep them that way.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/push/vapid.ts, src/components/pwa/PushOptIn.tsx
# Source: a P-256 VAPID key pair; mint one with web-push generate-vapid-keys or
# node crypto (raw uncompressed point, base64url, 87 chars). The public half is
# inlined into the client bundle by design; the notifications opt-in hides
# itself when it is unset. The PRIVATE half has no reader yet (no sender is
# built), but it must be minted together with the public one and kept: rows in
# push_subscriptions are only deliverable with the pair that subscribed them.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/whatsapp.ts, src/components/shared/WhatsAppFloat.tsx
# Source: the real business WhatsApp number, digits only, country code first.
# Fallback when unset: 972524635550, which is the number published by the
# WordPress store the live site still calls "Test Store". Set it.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WHATSAPP_PHONE=972500000000

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/contact-address.ts, src/app/(store)/contact/page.tsx
# Source: the mailbox that should receive contact-form and operator mail.
# CONTACT_TO=info@kenyonexpress.co.il


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cardcom payment gateway
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All four credentials come from Cardcom. A production terminal is issued on a
# phone call (03-9436100); a test terminal accepts real cards and settles
# nowhere, which is the failure this section exists to prevent.
#
# Contract: src/lib/payments/env.ts (loadCardcomEnv) and src/lib/env.ts.

# [required in production] Read by: src/lib/env.ts, src/lib/payments/env.ts, src/server/payments/invoices.ts
# Source: Cardcom > terminal details.
CARDCOM_TERMINAL_NUMBER=

# [required in production] Read by: src/lib/env.ts, src/lib/payments/env.ts
# Source: Cardcom > API settings.
CARDCOM_API_NAME=

# [required in production] Read by: src/lib/env.ts, src/lib/payments/env.ts
# Source: Cardcom > API settings.
CARDCOM_API_PASSWORD=

# [required in production] Read by: src/lib/payments/env.ts, the webhook route
# Source: chosen by you. `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# Cardcom does NOT sign its callbacks. This value rides on the IndicatorUrl as
# `?s=` and is compared in constant time. It is the only thing between the
# webhook and the open internet.
CARDCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/env.ts (acceptedWebhookSecrets)
# Source: the value CARDCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET had before you rotated it.
# Accepted on the way IN and never handed out. Rotating without it drops every
# callback for payment pages already open in a shopper's browser, and drops
# them with a 200, so Cardcom never retries. Remove once the longest checkout
# window has expired.
# CARDCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET_PREVIOUS=

# [required in production] Read by: src/lib/payments/env.ts (loadCardcomEnv)
# Source: you. The master switch for taking money, and it FAILS CLOSED:
# in production checkout is enabled only by the exact string "true". Missing or
# empty closes the pay button. Outside production the default is open.
CHECKOUT_ENABLED=false

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/env.ts, src/server/payments/invoices.ts
# WARNING: makes payments succeed without a card being charged. Never in production.
# CARDCOM_USE_MOCK=true

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/accounts.ts, src/lib/env.ts
# src/lib/env.ts fails the production boot when this is "true": sandbox left on
# in production means real orders settle against a test terminal, the shop looks
# healthy, and the money never arrives anywhere.
# CARDCOM_SANDBOX=false

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/accounts.ts
# Set to "true" to acknowledge deliberately-sandboxed accounts at load time
# rather than failing on the first customer.
# CARDCOM_ALLOW_SANDBOX=false

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/accounts.ts
# Source: you. A JSON array of extra merchant accounts, each needing at least
# id, terminalNumber and apiName. It may not redefine the platform account,
# which comes from CARDCOM_TERMINAL_NUMBER above.
# CARDCOM_ACCOUNTS=[]

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/accounts.ts
# Display label for the platform account. Fallback when unset: KenyonExpress
# CARDCOM_PLATFORM_LABEL=KenyonExpress

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/cardcom.ts
# Fallback when unset: the built-in legacy /Interface/*.aspx base URL. An empty
# string is NOT nullish, so `CARDCOM_API_BASE_URL=` is a different thing from
# leaving the line out; leave it out.
# CARDCOM_API_BASE_URL=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/cardcom.ts
# Cardcom document type codes. Fallbacks: credit note "3", coupon receipt "4".
# CARDCOM_CREDIT_NOTE_TYPE=3
# CARDCOM_COUPON_RECEIPT_TYPE=4

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/invoices/document.ts (resolveVatPercent)
# Fallback when unset: VAT_RATE_BP / 100 from src/lib/money.ts, currently 18.
# An unparseable or out-of-range value THROWS rather than falling back: issuing
# a day of documents at a silently wrong rate is not recoverable.
# INVOICE_VAT_PERCENT=18

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/invoices/provider.ts (resolveDocumentProviderId)
# Which company issues the tax documents. One of cardcom | green_invoice |
# icount | mock. Fallback when unset: cardcom, which is today's live behaviour
# and the only one with a working implementation -- the other three refuse with
# `provider_not_implemented` rather than pretending.
# AN UNRECOGNISED VALUE THROWS instead of falling back. A typo here would send
# every invoice to an account nobody is watching, and the money is already
# taken by then.
# INVOICE_PROVIDER=cardcom

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/cardcom.ts (cardcomTimeoutMs)
# Milliseconds any single Cardcom call may take. Fallback when unset: 15000,
# which is past Cardcom's normal response and under the platform's own request
# ceiling, leaving room for the one retry the read-only paths make.
# CARDCOM_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/threeds.ts
# 3-D Secure state sent to LowProfile.aspx. One of auto | enabled | disabled.
# Fallback when unset OR unrecognised: send nothing, and let the terminal's own
# dashboard configuration decide. That is the state every live payment so far
# has cleared under. An unrecognised value is logged (cardcom.threeds_state_unknown)
# rather than passed through, because a misspelled state on a legacy endpoint
# looks exactly like not sending one.
# CARDCOM_3DS_STATE=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/payments/threeds.ts
# Comma-separated decline codes that mean "this charge needs a 3DS challenge",
# compared exactly. Empty by default, and empty fails in the safe direction: an
# unrecognised decline stays a decline and the shopper is told so, rather than
# being bounced to a hosted challenge page for a card that was simply refused.
# The legacy interface's real codes are unconfirmed (see issues #41 and #42),
# which is why they are configuration and not constants.
# CARDCOM_3DS_REQUIRED_CODES=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Voucher QR signing
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [required in production] Read by: src/server/domain/vouchers/qr.ts, src/lib/env.ts
# Source: you. `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# NOT rotatable on a whim: every voucher already issued is signed with it.
# Rotating invalidates them all unless the old value moves to
# VOUCHER_QR_SECRET_PREVIOUS first.
VOUCHER_QR_SECRET=

# [optional] Read by: src/server/domain/vouchers/qr.ts
# Source: the previous VOUCHER_QR_SECRET. Verified on the way in, never used to
# sign. Remove once every voucher signed with it has expired.
# VOUCHER_QR_SECRET_PREVIOUS=

# [optional] Read by: src/server/domain/vouchers/issue.ts
# Key id stamped into newly issued QR payloads. Fallback when unset: v1
# VOUCHER_QR_KEY_ID=v1


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Order tracking link signing
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/orders/tracking-token.ts
# Source: you. `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# THE FALLBACK IS THE POINT, AND IT IS NOT A DEGRADED MODE. Unset, the signing
# key is derived by HMAC from the Supabase service key, which the server
# definitionally already has -- so the "track my order" link in a shipping mail
# works on a fresh deployment with nothing set. That is a direct answer to
# VOUCHER_QR_SECRET above, which was shipped as required, never set in
# production, and 500s every voucher scan to this day.
# Set this only to move the link onto its own key. Doing so invalidates
# outstanding tracking links, which is a non-event: the page is re-derivable
# from any later mail and carries no personal data.
# ORDER_TRACKING_SECRET=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduled jobs
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [required in production] Read by: every route under src/app/api/cron/, plus
# src/app/api/search/index-job/route.ts and src/app/api/search/index-dlq/route.ts
# Source: you. `openssl rand -hex 32`. The same value goes into the scheduler.
# It has no default: bearerMatches() compares against "" when it is unset, which
# never matches, so a missing CRON_SECRET closes every job rather than opening
# one. The symptom is ten schedules returning 401 forever, with no deploy error.
# The ten schedules themselves live in docs/CRON-EXTERNAL.md, not in vercel.json.
CRON_SECRET=

# [optional] Read by: src/app/api/cron/abandoned-cart/route.ts
# How many hours a cart must sit before it earns the FIRST reminder.
# Fallback: 2 (SECTIONS 26; it was 3 until 2026-09-09).
# ABANDONED_CART_HOURS=2
#
# [optional] Read by: src/app/api/cron/abandoned-cart/route.ts
# When the SECOND and last reminder is due, in hours from abandonment.
# Fallback: 24. The route spaces it from the FIRST MAIL rather than from the
# cart -- the gap it enforces is (this minus ABANDONED_CART_HOURS) -- so a cron
# that starts late cannot send both an hour apart. Needs pending/190; before
# that migration the deployed function ignores it and only one mail is ever
# sent per cart.
# ABANDONED_CART_SECOND_HOURS=24


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email (Resend)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [required] Read by: src/lib/health/checks.ts, src/lib/email/resend.ts,
#            src/lib/growth/resend.ts, src/app/api/cron/abandoned-cart/route.ts
# Source: Resend > API Keys. Without it no voucher ever reaches a buyer.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/email/resend.ts, src/lib/growth/resend.ts
# The transactional From address.
# Fallback when unset: KenyonExpress <noreply@kenyonexpress.co.il>
# The domain must be verified in Resend first, or every send is refused.
# EMAIL_FROM=KenyonExpress <noreply@kenyonexpress.co.il>

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/growth/resend.ts
# Marketing From address. Falls through to EMAIL_FROM when unset, so a deploy
# that set only EMAIL_FROM does not silently mail marketing from an unverified
# hardcoded address.
# RESEND_FROM=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/growth/resend.ts
# Source: Resend > Audiences. Required only for newsletter list operations.
# RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID=

# [optional] Read by: src/app/api/webhooks/resend/route.ts
# Source: Resend > Webhooks > the endpoint's signing secret. Starts `whsec_`.
# Fallback when unset: the webhook answers 401 to EVERYTHING, which is
# deliberate. Accepting unsigned posts would make an unauthenticated endpoint
# that writes the suppression list: anyone could post `email.bounced` for any
# address and stop that person receiving the coupon they paid for.
# Without it, bounces and complaints are never recorded and the suppression list
# stays empty, which is the state measured on 2026-09-09.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# [optional] Read by: src/server/actions/newsletter.ts
# Source: you. `openssl rand -hex 16`. Salts the stored consent IP hash.
# Fallback when unset: the empty string, which makes the hash reversible by
# anyone who can guess an IP. Set it wherever consent records are kept.
# CONSENT_IP_SALT=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observability: Sentry, logging, alerts
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [optional but effectively required] Read by: src/lib/observability/sentry.ts,
#            instrumentation-client.ts
# Source: Sentry > project kenyonexpress-web (EU region) > Client Keys (DSN).
# Read at BUILD time. A build without it produces a bundle whose SDK is
# `dsn: undefined` and reports nothing, silently.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=

# [optional] Read by: sentry.server.config.ts, sentry.edge.config.ts, src/lib/env.ts
# Server-side DSN. Same value as the public one unless you deliberately split them.
# SENTRY_DSN=

# [optional] Read by: sentry.server.config.ts, sentry.edge.config.ts / instrumentation-client.ts
# Environment tag on events. Fallback: NODE_ENV.
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production

# [optional] Read by: sentry.server.config.ts, sentry.edge.config.ts / instrumentation-client.ts
# Release tag. Fallback: VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA, which Vercel injects.
# SENTRY_RELEASE=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_RELEASE=

# [optional] Read by: next.config.ts, scripts/sentry-verify.mjs
# Source: Sentry > Settings > Auth Tokens. Build-time source map upload only.
# SENTRY_ORG=
# SENTRY_PROJECT=
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=

# [optional] Read by: next.config.ts
# Keeps uploaded source maps in the client bundle instead of deleting them.
# Leave unset: source maps served to the browser expose the server-rendered
# code paths alongside them.
# SENTRY_KEEP_SOURCEMAPS=

# [optional] Read by: src/app/api/debug/sentry/route.ts, src/app/debug/sentry/page.tsx
# Set to "true" to expose /debug/sentry, the fastest way to prove an event
# reaches the project from a real deployment. Turn it back off afterwards.
# SENTRY_DEBUG_ROUTES=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/observability/posthog.ts, src/components/analytics/PostHogReplay.tsx
# Source: PostHog > Project Settings > Project API Key. Read at BUILD time.
# Without it PostHog is entirely inert: no events, no session replay, no
# network calls, no SDK download. With it, events flow from the fetch-based
# capture path (pageviews, commerce funnel, referral landings, and the four
# server-side money events) and session replay records after cookie consent.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=

# [optional] Read by: the same two files. Defaults to https://us.i.posthog.com;
# set to https://eu.i.posthog.com for an EU-region project.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/observability/log.ts
# One of debug | info | warn | error. Fallback: info in production, debug otherwise.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/supabase/query-log-fetch.ts
# Milliseconds before a successful Supabase query logs db.query_slow at warn.
# Default 1500. The 10s hard deadline is SUPABASE_TIMEOUT_MS in timeout-fetch.ts.
# SUPABASE_SLOW_QUERY_MS=1500

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/observability/axiom.ts, src/lib/env.ts,
#            scripts/axiom/setup.mjs
# Source: Axiom > Settings > API Tokens. Needs ingest on the dataset below;
# the setup script additionally needs datasets + dashboards permissions.
# Both AXIOM_* must be set for the leg to engage; either one alone is treated
# as absent and every structured log line stays console-only. With both set,
# src/lib/observability/log.ts ships each line (already redacted) to Axiom,
# fire-and-forget. Query logs (db.query) are debug-level, so they reach Axiom
# only when LOG_LEVEL=debug; slow and failed queries ship at the default level.
# AXIOM_TOKEN=

# [optional] Read by: the same three files. The Axiom dataset name to ingest
# into, e.g. kenyon-logs. Create it and set 30-day retention plus the
# auth/payments/errors dashboards with: node scripts/axiom/setup.mjs
# AXIOM_DATASET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare Turnstile (bot challenge on signup, contact and checkout)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [optional] Read by: src/lib/fraud/turnstile.ts, src/lib/env.ts,
#            src/components/fraud/TurnstileWidget.tsx
# Source: Cloudflare dashboard > Turnstile > add a widget for the site domain.
#
# BOTH must be set for the challenge to be enforced. Either one alone is
# treated as absent and every form passes, because the widget only renders
# when the browser has the site key: a deployment with only the secret would
# receive no token from any real person and refuse every submission.
#
# With neither set, verification returns "allowed, not enforced". That is a
# deliberate fail-open - an absent bot control is a smaller problem than a
# shop that cannot take an order. Cloudflare's always-passing test pair
# (1x00000000000000000000AA / 1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA) is the
# quickest way to see the widget render without protecting anything.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/fraud/turnstile.ts, src/lib/env.ts (server only,
#            never sent to the browser). The secret half of the pair above,
#            posted to challenges.cloudflare.com/turnstile/v0/siteverify.
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/push/vapid.ts (browser), src/lib/push/web-push.ts.
# Web Push identity, RFC 8292. Generate ONCE with:
#     node -e "console.log(require('web-push').generateVAPIDKeys())"
# NEVER REGENERATE THE PAIR ONCE SUBSCRIPTIONS EXIST. A browser's subscription
# is bound to the public key it was minted against: a new pair orphans every row
# in push_subscriptions, sends start failing with 403, and every customer who
# opted in silently stops receiving anything.
# With these unset, the send leg skips WITHOUT counting an attempt and the
# opt-in UI does not offer a permission it cannot turn into a subscription.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/push/web-push.ts (server only, never sent to the
#            browser). The private half of the pair above.
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/push/web-push.ts. mailto: or https:, contacted by
#            a push service if this sender misbehaves. Defaults to the site URL.
# VAPID_SUBJECT=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/sms/twilio.ts. THE MASTER SWITCH FOR SMS.
# Exactly "true" enables it; anything else, including unset, leaves every send
# skipped without counting an attempt. It is a SECOND lock beside
# TWILIO_SMS_FROM because TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID and TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN are shared
# with WhatsApp and are already set wherever WhatsApp is configured.
# SMS_ENABLED=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/sms/twilio.ts. The registered Israeli sender ID
# or number, e.g. an approved alphanumeric like "KenyonExp".
# ISRAEL REQUIRES A REGISTERED SENDER. Messages from an unregistered one are
# dropped by the carriers WITHOUT A BOUNCE: Twilio reports delivered, the phone
# never rings, and nothing on either side says so. Do not set this to a number
# that has not been through the registration.
# A DIFFERENT VARIABLE from TWILIO_WHATSAPP_FROM on purpose: different senders,
# different registrations.
# TWILIO_SMS_FROM=

# [optional] Read by: src/app/api/webhooks/twilio-sms/route.ts. The webhook URL
# exactly as configured in the Twilio console, because the X-Twilio-Signature is
# computed over it -- and behind Vercel's proxy that is not request.url.
# Defaults to NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL + /api/webhooks/twilio-sms.
# TWILIO_SMS_WEBHOOK_URL=

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/observability/alert.ts
# Set to exactly "false" to silence outbound alerts. Anything else leaves them on.
# ALERTS_ENABLED=true

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/observability/alert.ts
# ntfy destination. Fallbacks: topic kenyon-ofir-limit, base https://ntfy.sh
# An ntfy topic is a public unauthenticated channel unless it is a private one,
# so treat the topic name itself as the only access control there is.
# NTFY_TOPIC=kenyon-ofir-limit
# NTFY_BASE_URL=https://ntfy.sh

# [optional] Read by: src/app/api/cron/health/route.ts
# The topic the health cron pages when a dependency is down. Falls back to NTFY_TOPIC.
# HEALTH_NTFY_TOPIC=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare R2 image storage
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source for all five: Cloudflare dashboard > R2 > your bucket > API tokens.
# Unset, the image pipeline falls back to Supabase Storage.
# Read by: src/lib/storage/r2.ts, src/lib/health/checks.ts,
#          src/server/actions/admin/images.ts, scripts/wp-import/06-media-sync.mjs

# [optional] R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# [optional] R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# [optional] R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# [optional] R2_BUCKET=
# [optional] R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://cdn.kenyonexpress.co.il


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Search: Meilisearch and the index pipeline
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset, /search falls back to a Postgres LIKE query. That works and is slower.
# Read by: src/lib/search-server.ts, src/lib/health/checks.ts,
#          src/app/api/search/suggest/route.ts, src/lib/search/meili-settings.ts

# [optional] Source: your Meilisearch host.
# MEILISEARCH_HOST=https://search.kenyonexpress.co.il
# [optional] Source: Meilisearch > keys. Use a search-only key in the browser path.
# MEILISEARCH_API_KEY=
# [optional] Fallback: products
# MEILISEARCH_INDEX=products
# [optional] Fallback: brands. The derived brand-autocomplete index, rebuilt by
# scripts/setup-meilisearch.mjs from distinct products.brand values.
# MEILISEARCH_BRANDS_INDEX=brands
# [optional] Read by: src/lib/search/meilisearch.ts. Fallback: coupons
# The coupon subset of the catalogue, for surfaces that search only deals. The
# products index still holds the WHOLE catalogue including coupons, so site-wide
# search keeps finding them.
# MEILISEARCH_COUPONS_INDEX=coupons
# [optional] Local dev only: docker-compose.yml boots Meilisearch with this as
# its master key (it doubles as MEILISEARCH_API_KEY against localhost:7700).
# MEILI_MASTER_KEY=kenyon-dev-master-key-change-me

# [optional] Read by: src/lib/search/qstash.ts, scripts/qstash-sync.mjs
# Source: Upstash > QStash > Details. Drives the async reindex queue and its DLQ.
# QSTASH_TOKEN=
# QSTASH_CURRENT_SIGNING_KEY=
# QSTASH_NEXT_SIGNING_KEY=

# [optional] Read by: src/app/api/webhooks/products/route.ts
# Source: you. `openssl rand -hex 32`. Signs product-change webhooks from senders
# that can sign; the route falls back to CRON_SECRET bearer auth for those that cannot.
# SEARCH_WEBHOOK_SECRET=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rate limiting (Upstash Redis)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset, the limiter falls back to the Postgres `check_rate_limit` the app
# already shipped with. That fallback is real and tested, not a stub, so leaving
# this section empty degrades throughput rather than removing the limits.
# Read by: src/lib/env.ts, src/lib/rate-limit/upstash.ts, src/lib/rate-limit/limiter.ts

# [optional] Source: Upstash > your Redis database > REST API.
# BOTH must be set for the Upstash path to engage. Either one alone is treated
# as absent, so a half-finished configuration degrades to Postgres instead of
# failing every request that passes through a limiter.
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# [optional] Milliseconds. Fallback when unset: 1000, in src/lib/rate-limit/upstash.ts.
# A limiter that hangs holds the request open, which is worse than a limiter
# that gives up and lets the Postgres path answer.
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TIMEOUT_MS=1000


